WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.290
I'm Jenna Fisher, and I'm Angela Kinsey.

2
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.150
We were on The Office together, and we're best friends.

3
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.270
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

4
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.790
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

5
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.970
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of VFF stories.

6
00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.990
We're The Office Lady 6.0.

7
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.480
Hello, everyone.

8
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.540
Hello.

9
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.160
Lady, we have a great day today.

10
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.680
We have another revisited deep dive episode of Office Ladies.

11
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:35.460
Yes.

12
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.740
Today, we are going to go back over the second half of Season 3.

13
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.380
We're going to get to your questions, your comments about all the things we missed.

14
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:48.460
We're doing everything from back from vacation to the job.

15
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.560
Yeah, we are.

16
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.820
And I want you to know, I'm holding what looks like a small phone book in my hand

17
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.560
because we went through so many questions and comments,

18
00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.460
and Jenna and I both jotted a ton down, and we're excited to talk to you guys today.

19
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.480
Yeah, it's like 19, 20 pages, I think.

20
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.780
It is, it is.

21
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.360
But first, we have some exciting news.

22
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.760
We are going to start this off with a deep dive with our awesome cinematographer and director,

23
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.080
Randall Einhorn.

24
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.040
Randall was the cinematographer for 64 episodes,

25
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.940
and he directed The Accountant's Webisodes, as well as 15 episodes of The Office.

26
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.240
This man knows this show inside and out.

27
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.880
We are so excited to talk to him.

28
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.180
Guys, Randall was one of our original visionaries for the show.

29
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.980
He really helped shape the world, the mockumentary style.

30
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.860
He has also been amazing.

31
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.520
I've been reaching out to him.

32
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.160
You've been reaching out to him, Angela.

33
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:47.620
You might remember he gave us all of the amazing details about Michael's car going into the lake.

34
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.180
So, we are really excited to get this deep dive interview with him for the pod.

35
00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.020
So, you guys, when we all got on the Zoom with Randall, we were so excited to see each other.

36
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:01.660
It was the first time the three of us, I mean, Jenna, I don't even know the last time the three of us were together.

37
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.620
So, we got super excited.

38
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.340
We did all of our hellos and forgot to hit record.

39
00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:08.500
Yes.

40
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.800
Sam was like, guys, are you recording?

41
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.180
And I was like, oh, crap.

42
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:13.160
Yeah.

43
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.820
So, when you hear this interview, we're going to jump right in.

44
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.260
Because we talked for 20 minutes without hitting record.

45
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:20.900
Yeah.

46
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:21.940
Oh, Sam.

47
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.000
Sam, Sam.

48
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.100
Every week, you're like, and should you hit record?

49
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.080
Every week.

50
00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.740
Sam, can you play that interview?

51
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:39.000
Randall, we always start by asking people basically the same question, which is, how did you come to be on The Office?

52
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:50.180
I was doing a reality TV show, kind of an extreme sports thing, where I had to ski with Sean White and some other legendary skier.

53
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.100
And I had to keep up with these guys skiing and film these extreme skiers in Jackson Hole.

54
00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.660
It was kind of an extreme sports thing for Ben Silverman.

55
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.420
And Ben, at that time, apparently decided, that's the guy.

56
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.620
Wait, when he decided this, were you flying down a mountain holding a camera?

57
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:06.160
Yeah.

58
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.840
I would say that's the guy, too.

59
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:21.200
I would say that's the guy for anything, but it is a curious jump to go from guy filming on skis to filming a quiet, contained office cubicle.

60
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.000
Yes.

61
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.180
I went from outdoor adventure guy to the office guy.

62
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.760
Ben convinced Greg that that's the guy.

63
00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:35.240
I remember Greg and I met at a Starbucks, and Greg said, just meet me at the Starbucks on Santa Monica.

64
00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:41.860
I was new to Los Angeles, and I went to one of the 50 Starbucks in Los Angeles.

65
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.540
And I finally got in touch with Greg, and I showed up like an hour late.

66
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.740
And Greg is still sitting there, just waiting for me.

67
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.500
And we had a fantastic meeting, and it was wonderful.

68
00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.580
And that's where Greg and I really hit it off.

69
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.980
Did you bring Matt Sohn into the picture?

70
00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.520
He was the camera operator.

71
00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:00.960
Yes.

72
00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:02.380
Had you worked together before?

73
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.100
Yeah, you guys knew each other, right?

74
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:11.100
Matt and I knew each other from the outdoor adventure world, and also from Survivor and Eco Challenge.

75
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.720
And we filmed scenes together.

76
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:19.700
And when you're filming a scene together in kind of this verite style, you know, documentary style,

77
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:26.200
you really ascertain really quickly who's looking out for you and who's doing the compliment of what you're doing.

78
00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.480
And that verite dance, Matt and I did really, really well.

79
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:35.420
I remember just a couple times that we've shot together, and he's like, oh, he gets what I'm doing, and I get what he's doing.

80
00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:41.060
And we can look at each other out of, you know, out of the eye that's not behind the camera and communicate.

81
00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:46.620
And I just felt Matt was the best person for me to do that dance with.

82
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:58.420
Randall, were you so bored to be inside of this cubicle warehouse office space with these fluorescent lights after all you guys had shot?

83
00:04:58.680 --> 00:04:59.020
No.

84
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.260
I mean, we had shot.

85
00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.260
We had always shot things that we just reacted to.

86
00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:04.480
That's what cinema verite is.

87
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.060
You're just reacting.

88
00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.420
You're just gleaning what's happening around you.

89
00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:15.560
And it was really cool to kind of reverse engineer that and actually have to make it look like you were just getting it.

90
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.520
I never really worked in narrative stuff before.

91
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.880
I'd always done, you know, reality and documentaries.

92
00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:28.180
And it was really cool to make scripted stuff look like a documentary because it took another layer of finesse.

93
00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.280
You know, normally you're just sitting there and you're filming what happens, and you're just sitting there on a tripod.

94
00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:41.840
Had we been sitting there on dollies or on a tripod, and I just know what everybody's going to say, and I'm just doing pans, yeah, I would have been bored.

95
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:48.860
But to be able to react and, you know, and play a character.

96
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:49.380
You were.

97
00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:50.760
You were a character.

98
00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:51.500
You were.

99
00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:52.660
Thank you.

100
00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:53.200
Yeah.

101
00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:54.660
You know what, though?

102
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.220
I did always think of you guys.

103
00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:02.840
I mean, Jenna and I have talked about this because we knew, like, some of the adventures you and Matt had had, you had shared with us on set.

104
00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:08.780
We always sort of chuckled when we would have a situation like a lit candle on stage.

105
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:12.320
And Kelly Cantley, our AD, would be like, safety meeting.

106
00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:14.420
We're going to light a candle.

107
00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:22.600
And, like, Jenna and I knowing that you had a camera strapped to you, like, going down, like, some, like, boat in a river in Borneo.

108
00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.020
Like, Randall's like, okay, lit candle.

109
00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:25.500
Got it.

110
00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:27.100
Yeah.

111
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:28.220
Yeah.

112
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.600
Safety meetings over a candle are quite, they're really something.

113
00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:32.920
It still gets me to this day.

114
00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:39.080
But I think it's all under the name of safety because safety never takes a holiday, so.

115
00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.140
That's right.

116
00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:41.640
Yeah.

117
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:49.460
Well, Randall, what were some of the early conversations you had with Greg about the look of the show or preserving the documentary style?

118
00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.700
Did you guys have sort of a game plan how you were going to shoot and what it was going to look like?

119
00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:54.640
Very much so.

120
00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.740
I saw the original office, you know, the British version.

121
00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:00.260
And when Greg and I met, I pitched him this idea.

122
00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.340
I said it's kind of like a tofu hot dog.

123
00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:10.420
So it's like good food that's wrapped like bad food.

124
00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:19.080
And it's kind of you're putting this fake veneer on it so that people kind of lean in.

125
00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:30.380
And the one thing that we always did on shows like Survivor and all reality that I ever did in documentaries is when a conversation is intimate, you back away from it.

126
00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.220
You don't go to it like normal TV.

127
00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:40.420
You back away and you back away and you back away because that makes the viewer think that they're privy to something that they shouldn't necessarily be privy to.

128
00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.900
So they feel in on the joke.

129
00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.480
They feel in on the moment.

130
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.880
And I think it adds a level of intimacy that is really nice.

131
00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.100
As an actor, Randall, I loved how you would back that camera off for those intimate moments,

132
00:07:54.100 --> 00:08:01.620
because one of the hard things as an actor is blocking out the camera that is inches away from you.

133
00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:04.780
So and it was very freeing.

134
00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:13.040
It was one of my favorite experiences, really, is how private those moments really felt because you guys were further away.

135
00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:13.680
Yeah.

136
00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:14.400
Yes.

137
00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:14.800
Yes.

138
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.120
I've been on shows now since the office.

139
00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:28.720
I did this scene and the camera was so close that when the other actor paused and talking, I could just hear because the camera was that close.

140
00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:32.860
And you're trying to, like, say, OK, I'm not hearing that mechanical noise.

141
00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:34.640
I'm in the moment with this other person.

142
00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:35.360
Yeah.

143
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.120
I think there's something always to be said for just giving the set to actors.

144
00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.720
It's something I as a director now, I always try to back away and just let it be.

145
00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.360
It shouldn't be my my environment.

146
00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:49.420
It should be the actor's environment.

147
00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:53.220
That's something I absolutely learned on the office from some of the great directors.

148
00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.540
Well, as an actor, I love it.

149
00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:57.680
Yeah, me too.

150
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:05.020
Randall, can you tell us a little bit about how you would prep a scene with a director when you were in the role of cinematographer?

151
00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.720
Like a standard scene in the bullpen and you're having to cover a bunch of reactions.

152
00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:11.500
How would you prep that?

153
00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:17.220
For the office, we would basically just tend to rehearse a scene and the actors would do what the actors would do.

154
00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:19.920
And Matt and I would just stand there like this.

155
00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:20.740
And this is our camera.

156
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.720
And Matt and I would be looking at each other like, OK, I got this.

157
00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:24.340
I got Jenna.

158
00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:25.100
Great.

159
00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:25.800
You've got Jim.

160
00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:26.220
Great.

161
00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:28.860
I'm going to do a swing to Jim after Jenna's line.

162
00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.260
So I'm going to connect them like that.

163
00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.020
And Matt would duck.

164
00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.000
And I would get that.

165
00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:38.260
And then I'd come back up and we and we would we would just kind of both stand there.

166
00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:48.300
And if you look at shows like like Survivor, let's say, where you have two people talking and you have the what we call dirty overs.

167
00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.960
You don't have the person necessarily clean.

168
00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.160
You try to connect them.

169
00:09:52.340 --> 00:10:01.360
And that was something that we always tried to do is to show that human connection by placing both characters in both of our frames.

170
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:05.820
So Matt and I would have to just frame each other out.

171
00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:10.380
And sometimes that means you need to back up and get a little bit longer on the lens.

172
00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:14.660
So you see you don't see Matt's shoulder or Matt's knee or whatever.

173
00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:19.560
So, yeah, we would just sit there and we would react to it.

174
00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:25.220
And Matt and I would just do this to do this dance where we would figure out what what our coverage was.

175
00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.840
And I remember Harold Ramis directing.

176
00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.080
And it was it was the coolest moment.

177
00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:31.180
Harold came in.

178
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:37.580
I mean, he prepped like an extra week because he was so excited about doing the show, which I just everybody.

179
00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:39.200
I remember Steve was giddy.

180
00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.340
We were in Steve's office and doing a scene.

181
00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.200
He was like, that's Harold Ramis right there.

182
00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:45.220
Harold Ramis.

183
00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:47.140
Yeah.

184
00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.740
But I mean, Harold really, really prepped.

185
00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.720
And he had all those plans like I had just meticulously drawn out.

186
00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.340
And I remember we rehearsed the first scene.

187
00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:03.160
And Matt and I just kind of stood there and did this and that and talked it through.

188
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.180
And I looked at him and he looked at me and we figured out who was going to get what.

189
00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.160
And Harold came over.

190
00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:12.680
He says, I should just get rid of this.

191
00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:12.880
Right.

192
00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:18.360
I'm like, no, Harold, this really it's such an honor to be working with you.

193
00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:19.520
And yeah, yeah.

194
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:20.360
Don't shit me, kid.

195
00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.380
I should get rid of this plan.

196
00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:21.640
Right.

197
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:23.320
This is what you did was way better.

198
00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:27.060
I'm like, well, that's a it's a great road map, Harold.

199
00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.460
And and then, you know, we did one more scene.

200
00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.100
And I remember watching him looking at him.

201
00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.000
He got my attention.

202
00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.920
He just chucked his plans in Pam's trash bin.

203
00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.280
I remember exactly where we were.

204
00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:37.720
Yeah.

205
00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:45.540
Harold Ramis threw his shot plans into Pam's trash bin because he thought you nailed it.

206
00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.840
That that's great, Randall.

207
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.480
That is that for me, I'd be like, someone go get those out of the trash.

208
00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:52.400
I'm saving those.

209
00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:54.300
Yeah.

210
00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:55.100
Yeah.

211
00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:59.240
Well, I have to say, I was really curious what your answer was going to be to that question,

212
00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:06.980
because my memory is that for the most part, it was you and Matt figuring out the shots

213
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.160
during our rehearsal.

214
00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:15.700
And the directors were more focused on just actor performance, giving us notes, making

215
00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:20.360
sure we landed the jokes, making sure that the writers got their alternate jokes in.

216
00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:26.180
And really, there was this huge trust that you guys knew how to shoot the show.

217
00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:31.860
The directors didn't, for the most part, like micromanage you in any way.

218
00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:38.200
I don't recall anybody ever telling me, get to him on this line or miss this line.

219
00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:43.600
It was always, and you know, when I went, and it's something I still do, when I'm talking

220
00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:48.300
to cameras, I talk to a camera operator like I'm talking to an actor.

221
00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.680
I will say, you think this, but you also suspect that she might do that.

222
00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:59.660
And that kind of gives the camera a little bit of a point of view, which for The Office

223
00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:00.440
is paramount.

224
00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:04.940
For other shows, I think is also interesting, because you're trusting the intuition of the

225
00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:10.320
camera operator, and you're giving them the basis to add some finesse to a scene, which

226
00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.080
I think is always really interesting.

227
00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:13.520
That's beautiful.

228
00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:14.720
I love that.

229
00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:20.920
Well, you know, Randall, one of the things you guys were also so great at was catching these

230
00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:22.560
moments that weren't scripted.

231
00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.420
I think that the way we filmed it added a level of authenticity, as did the improvisational

232
00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:27.840
moments.

233
00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.720
And whether we used improvisational moments or not, it still put everybody in that space

234
00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:33.940
of this is happening.

235
00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:34.940
This is a real thing.

236
00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:36.180
There's an immediacy to this.

237
00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:37.400
It's not a rehearsed thing.

238
00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.700
It's something that is happening in front of us and around us.

239
00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:45.240
And for you all to be part of that was awesome.

240
00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:49.640
It was amazing to see, you know, characters never drop character.

241
00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:54.940
They just kept Kevin, kept being Kevin, and Angela kept being Angela.

242
00:13:55.660 --> 00:14:02.780
And adding those moments in, to me, made it feel like a living thing and made it feel

243
00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:09.600
real and organic in a really meaningful and significant way that affected the overall

244
00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:10.080
outcome.

245
00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:11.580
That is so poetic.

246
00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:16.460
That is the best answer I have ever heard about improv on a set.

247
00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:21.920
Well, Randall, I know that we kept you in that office under the fluorescent light most

248
00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.840
of the time, but occasionally we did cram you into the backseat of a car or we put you

249
00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:28.560
on a boat.

250
00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:31.620
What was it like?

251
00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.840
What were those shoot days for you like?

252
00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:42.000
A shot note in a lot of awkward places, Einstein said, in the midst of difficulty lies opportunity.

253
00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:46.340
And the harder it got, the better it got.

254
00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:51.780
Like I just rewatched that scene in the car where we're driving to the lake.

255
00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.420
And the whole time I was just reacting to it.

256
00:14:55.420 --> 00:15:01.460
And I remember those, you know, in the midst of everything happening, as difficult as it

257
00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:06.220
was with the water coming in and he cramped in the backseat, when the GPS spoke, I panned

258
00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:08.320
to the GPS because it was a character in the scene.

259
00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:15.220
So those opportunities, I think, add another element in a really, really interesting way.

260
00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:22.180
But like Michael's Sebring, which he was so proud of, it was a coupe.

261
00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:22.900
It was a two-door.

262
00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:30.040
So the backseat, although it's a four-seater car or a five-seater car, it wasn't a two-seater

263
00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:34.620
plus a cameraman very comfortably because I remember the camera came up higher than the

264
00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.980
back window because it was such a cool car, Michael, that the back window had such a slope

265
00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:43.160
to it that my camera would always hit the back of the window.

266
00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:48.260
And there was a hump there where the transmission went through and there's really kind of no place

267
00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:49.280
for your legs to go.

268
00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:54.860
So you just kind of tie yourself in a knot and hopefully find some opportunity by that

269
00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:55.300
discomfort.

270
00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.240
You know, I get, I get car sick.

271
00:15:58.440 --> 00:15:59.700
I get really car sick.

272
00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:00.140
What?

273
00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:01.040
Yeah.

274
00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:02.840
Every time I got out of that car.

275
00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:04.980
How in the world do you do your job?

276
00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:05.960
Yeah.

277
00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:06.720
Oh my God.

278
00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:08.120
You get really car sick?

279
00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:08.620
Go on.

280
00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:10.260
Randall, I had no idea.

281
00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:15.240
Because when we, so many times when we filmed in cars, we just looped, we did a circle and

282
00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.720
I would get nauseous just doing the circle.

283
00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:18.900
Yeah.

284
00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:21.000
Let alone only with one eye.

285
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:22.300
Yeah.

286
00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:23.560
No, I get really car sick.

287
00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:28.400
And I remember every time Chris, Chris and Ed were helping me get to get the heavy camera

288
00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.060
out and helping me come out.

289
00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:31.060
They're like, how are you doing?

290
00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:31.480
You all right?

291
00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:31.980
You okay?

292
00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:33.660
I'm like, yeah, real good.

293
00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:37.320
And I would all, you know, it lasts a couple hours, but.

294
00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:41.620
We had, we had so many, we had so many driving scenes, Randall.

295
00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:42.880
I never knew this.

296
00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:43.820
Yeah.

297
00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:44.860
Oh my gosh.

298
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:45.240
Yeah.

299
00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:47.080
I was getting a little green at times back there.

300
00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:47.900
I was just kind of sweating.

301
00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:56.400
Well, thank you because it really did produce some amazing, amazing things.

302
00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:58.080
That was fun.

303
00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:05.280
I know we're sort of talking about like difficult camera positions and stuff, but Randall, you

304
00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:06.820
have to share with everybody.

305
00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:10.780
You introduced us to the Poodie Scootie.

306
00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.360
The Poodie Scootie.

307
00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.220
But did you invent that?

308
00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:16.040
Was that something Dale made for you?

309
00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:17.580
Dale made it for us.

310
00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:23.680
I, we were like, I would love to be able to scoot around the office at actor level and

311
00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:26.580
move, you know, not like on a dolly.

312
00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:28.400
And we just wanted to be quick and nimble.

313
00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:34.760
And also so that I can react to it as opposed to communicating to a grip that I need to move

314
00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:36.460
back a foot because I'm going to pan to the left.

315
00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:37.860
We never would have gotten there.

316
00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.100
So Dale built those and it was great.

317
00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:43.560
That thing worked really, really well.

318
00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:44.300
It was so fun.

319
00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:45.960
It was like a rolling box.

320
00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:48.840
And then they eventually got you a little cushion on top of it.

321
00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:49.840
Right?

322
00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:50.100
Cushion.

323
00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:50.860
Those were the days.

324
00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:51.820
Yeah, those were the days.

325
00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:52.160
Cushion.

326
00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:53.740
And you could spin around.

327
00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:57.520
It was like, kind of like a stool, but a little better than a stool for you because I think

328
00:17:57.520 --> 00:17:58.620
it was like square.

329
00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:00.040
Yeah.

330
00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:04.360
Did you take the Pootie Scootie with you when you left the office?

331
00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:05.040
I didn't.

332
00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:05.680
I didn't.

333
00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:06.500
You didn't?

334
00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:07.620
No.

335
00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:08.840
No.

336
00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:13.120
And since, and since then I've had other Pootie Scooties built for me and they just weren't

337
00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:13.780
the same.

338
00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:24.800
I feel like something I want to tell our audience is how incredibly rare it is for a cinematographer

339
00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.620
to also operate the camera.

340
00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:32.200
And it sounds like, Randall, you fought so hard to always keep that camera in your hands.

341
00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:33.540
You did not want to put it on sticks.

342
00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.060
You did not want to put it on a dolly.

343
00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:36.680
You were the operator.

344
00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:44.760
And in other projects I've done, it really is usually a cinematographer directing a camera

345
00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:49.640
operator and they don't put their hands on the camera so much.

346
00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:51.320
It was like this really cool thing.

347
00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:51.960
Yeah.

348
00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:57.260
I think there's always an element of disconnect because on traditional shows, there's like

349
00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:02.500
eight to 10 people just, you know, who are looking after two cameras.

350
00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:05.920
And on our show, our camera department was four.

351
00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:14.500
It was me and Matt and Chris and Ed and Matt and I pulled our own focus and we pulled our

352
00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:15.140
own iris.

353
00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:17.360
I would set the look for the cameras.

354
00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:20.880
You know, I would program the cameras, what we wanted them to look like and how we wanted

355
00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:21.460
them to match.

356
00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:24.320
And then the rest of the stuff was just worked out in poSt.

357
00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:29.760
But Matt and I thought it was really important to pull our own focus and pull our own iris.

358
00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:35.680
I also think there's, again, a level of intimacy when the operator,

359
00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:39.920
is part of the scene and is also listening to the scene.

360
00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:43.940
And that's something that doesn't happen on a lot of shows is quite often a lot of shows,

361
00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:45.640
the DP doesn't listen to the words.

362
00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.400
And I wonder, how do you know what to do?

363
00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:49.140
Yeah.

364
00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:55.360
Because coming up in documentaries, I didn't know what to shoot until my sound man put the

365
00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:57.780
boom up and said, this is a relevant conversation.

366
00:19:57.780 --> 00:19:59.260
You should be shooting this, idiot.

367
00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:00.100
Don't shoot that.

368
00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:04.960
So I still, hey, when I operate, I wear an earpiece.

369
00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.920
So I'm hearing what's going on so that I can finesse what I'm doing.

370
00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:12.260
Now, Randall, sometimes on our set, things got a little physical.

371
00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:14.340
Who ran into you?

372
00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.080
Who did you collide with the most?

373
00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.100
You know, I think I sent myself to the floor in the fire one.

374
00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.740
I remember that I was backing up through the kitchen and I fell.

375
00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.560
And I think I hit the ground, but I'm not real sure.

376
00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:33.100
But I'm a 200-pound human being and the camera is 38 pounds.

377
00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:39.020
And I remember Chris Workman, who's a very strong person, I think he picked me up with

378
00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.260
one hand and I just kept rolling.

379
00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:43.820
He just picked me up with one hand.

380
00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:44.960
I'm like, oh, did I fall?

381
00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:47.800
And I just kept going with the scene because I'm like, I think I fell.

382
00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:51.780
And Randall, that's the footage that stayed in.

383
00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:52.600
Yes.

384
00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:56.080
We used the take of you falling and getting back up.

385
00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:57.040
It was incredible.

386
00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:02.300
I remember being there and seeing it out of the corner of my eye and thinking, should

387
00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:03.640
we, are we still going?

388
00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:05.420
Because Randall is on the floor.

389
00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:06.840
Oh, no, Randall's up.

390
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:07.440
Randall's up.

391
00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:07.760
All right.

392
00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:08.680
We're going to continue.

393
00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:09.780
Yep.

394
00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:14.140
That was Chris Workman just lifting me up like a little rag doll and letting me go.

395
00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:15.180
I was just back on my feet.

396
00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:22.020
Anyway, one thing I remember is in Benihana, I fell on the Pootie Scootie.

397
00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.740
I remember the last thing I saw was Steve just going, no.

398
00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:29.460
And it was always that.

399
00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.660
It was always Steve like, no, I got you.

400
00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:34.700
I want to ask this question.

401
00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:42.160
In addition to saving your life in that scene, Chris Workman also used to bring you this drink

402
00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:43.160
on set.

403
00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:45.780
And Angela and I need to know more about it.

404
00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:48.440
It looked like it was like fruit and water.

405
00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:50.340
What was this drink?

406
00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:52.760
Was this your special camera operator fuel?

407
00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:58.100
You see, that's the difference between being out in the hot sun in Thailand and shooting

408
00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:00.880
people on a beach and being in the office.

409
00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:04.460
I would not get the same type of beverages.

410
00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:05.800
I'll tell you.

411
00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:08.680
You know, that was one of the perks.

412
00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:09.380
One of the perks.

413
00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.980
I mean, season three, I would have, I would have them.

414
00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:13.260
I like bubbly water.

415
00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:13.940
Yes.

416
00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:15.780
I was going to say, I remember it was fizzy.

417
00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:16.620
Yeah.

418
00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:18.540
I sound like such a fancy man.

419
00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:19.940
I like bubbly water.

420
00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:28.040
And I would have Chris bruise the fruit and put it in the, in my water for me.

421
00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:31.820
And so I would have real fruit flavored bubbly water.

422
00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:34.280
Now, if that's not living, that's not a sign of success.

423
00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:35.360
I don't know what is.

424
00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.460
I love it.

425
00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:40.360
Chris made me one one time because I was like, Chris, where do you keep bringing Randall?

426
00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:40.920
I want one.

427
00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:44.000
And then he just sort of slyly slid, like slid one on my desk.

428
00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.120
I was like, it was so refreshing.

429
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:48.560
I stand by it, Randall.

430
00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:49.860
I think it's a great move.

431
00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:54.220
And you know, you're not, you're not too fancy on your pootie scootie with your bubbly water.

432
00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:56.480
Yeah.

433
00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:57.060
Okay.

434
00:22:57.220 --> 00:22:59.040
Randall, we do ask our guests this.

435
00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:03.760
Did you take anything from the set when the show wrapped, when you were done?

436
00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:05.280
We all took a little something.

437
00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:06.180
Did you take something?

438
00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:07.100
I didn't.

439
00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:08.120
I didn't take anything.

440
00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:08.740
What?

441
00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:09.520
Randall.

442
00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.460
I was always hoping for a season 10.

443
00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:15.640
So I just didn't want to preserve that and make sure that I didn't screw it up.

444
00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:19.180
And if I took a pencil holder that we, you know, we wouldn't come back.

445
00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:20.760
I didn't, I didn't take anything.

446
00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:21.580
I swear.

447
00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:26.040
In hindsight now, if you could have taken something, what would you have taken?

448
00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.160
Yeah, I spent my plaque with my name on it.

449
00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:29.440
I think that was pretty cool.

450
00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:30.520
I don't know what I would take.

451
00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:32.300
Toaster oven from the fire.

452
00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:34.000
Yeah, there you go.

453
00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:34.700
Nice.

454
00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.220
Well, we should share with our listeners.

455
00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:42.840
And I think, you know, a lot of Office Ladies fans will know this, that Randall, you went

456
00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:48.740
on and now are a prolific director and you're directing all kinds of amazing shows.

457
00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:52.240
And I got to work with you post office on Wilfred.

458
00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:53.740
That was really fun.

459
00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:55.640
Weird little show.

460
00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:58.860
Weird little show, but such a fun group of people.

461
00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:02.040
I was just there one day, but I loved the vibe on that set.

462
00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:07.720
But you are, you are just out there crushing it as the kids say.

463
00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:13.780
Randall, do you have any upcoming projects or anything we can share with everyone to check

464
00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:14.040
out?

465
00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:16.200
I don't direct as much as I used to.

466
00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.940
I have an overall development deal.

467
00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:20.980
So I'm developing for ABC.

468
00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:22.780
Development is very cool.

469
00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:23.680
I love development.

470
00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:26.600
So we will be looking for your projects coming out.

471
00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:28.120
And I can't wait.

472
00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:32.440
Randall, your sparkly blue eye is a delight to see both of them.

473
00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:35.960
We normally saw one of them, but both of them are wonderful to see.

474
00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:37.320
Yes, Randall.

475
00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:40.660
Thank you so much for taking this time to deep dive with us.

476
00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.100
I've been so looking forward to this.

477
00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:46.780
And thank you too for letting us email you.

478
00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:52.420
And for when you've hopped on the phone with me to tell me tidbits behind the scenes, you've

479
00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:54.580
helped made this podcast even better.

480
00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:55.400
So thank you.

481
00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:57.320
Thank you.

482
00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:00.020
It's been, it was a really special time.

483
00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.740
And to get to relive it, I'll do that any chance I get.

484
00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:03.520
So keep calling.

485
00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:07.340
I was about to say, Jenna, we're not done calling him.

